WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.089
A sensor records a signal. Calling that signal an early

00:00:04.089 --> 00:00:07.364
warning of heart failure is a much larger claim.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.443
Heart failure is not a single signal. Physiological

00:00:19.443 --> 00:00:22.438
changes appear through movements, rhythms and

00:00:22.438 --> 00:00:24.435
measurements interpreted in context.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.805
Non-invasive and minimally invasive sensors can produce

00:00:34.805 --> 00:00:38.538
information about cardiac and circulatory physiology

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.649
without requiring identical clinical procedures.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.496
The chapter surveys sensor technologies intended to

00:00:49.496 --> 00:00:52.965
help identify or predict heart-failure trajectories,

00:00:52.965 --> 00:00:55.277
with different technological trade-offs.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:04.408
Motion, noise, timing and patient variability all

00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:08.366
influence interpretation. A useful measurement is not

00:01:08.366 --> 00:01:11.193
automatically a useful clinical decision.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.616
Performance depends on the patient population,

00:01:18.616 --> 00:01:21.782
reference standard and timing. Distinguish a

00:01:21.782 --> 00:01:24.948
physiological measurement from a validated prediction.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.465
Predictive performance needs evidence from realistic

00:01:34.465 --> 00:01:38.481
populations and robust external testing; practical

00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:41.157
accessibility matters alongside technical

00:01:41.157 --> 00:01:41.827
sophistication.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.365
A device becomes clinically useful through evidence,

00:01:48.365 --> 00:01:51.280
not through the elegance of its dashboard.
